from docx import Document
from docx.shared import Pt
from docx.oxml import OxmlElement
from docx.text.paragraph import Paragraph
import pandas as pd

d=Document('Group_31_Term_Paper.docx')
replacements={
'Digital financial services can help': 'Digital financial services can expand access to payments and savings, while safe use remains essential. Using the World Bank Global Findex 2025 India microdata, this study analyzes 3,000 adult respondents interviewed in 2024. Survey-weighted account ownership was 89.0% and 48.5% made or received a digital payment. Among mobile-phone owners, 13.1% of digital-payment users reported an unsolicited call or message asking for money, compared with 4.3% of non-users. A weighted exploratory logistic model yields an adjusted odds ratio of 3.45 for payment use; its uncertainty estimates do not incorporate the full survey design. Among phone owners 57.7% reported a device lock. These associations are not causal and unsolicited requests are not necessarily financial fraud. The findings motivate a proposed accessible safety and reporting service, whose impact and commercial viability require testing.',
'The research questions are:': 'The research questions are: (1) How do account ownership and digital payments differ? (2) Are digital-payment users more likely to report unsolicited money requests? (3) Does device locking appear associated with sending money in response? (4) Which demographic groups show differences in adoption and device security? We use weighted descriptive statistics and an exploratory adjusted model. The cross-sectional data cannot establish causal effects or the sequence of exposure and adoption.',
'This paper is a descriptive secondary-data study.': 'This paper analyzes the World Bank India Global Findex 2025 microdata, version IND_2024_FINDEX_v02_M, with 3,000 respondents interviewed in 2024 [2]. The file includes a survey weight but no published strata or primary sampling unit identifiers. Population shares use wgt; all sample sizes are unweighted. For binary derived indicators, 0 includes no, do not know and refusal as coded by the World Bank. For raw connectivity questions, only yes and no responses are included; don’t know, refusal and missing values are excluded. The survey questions on phone locks and unsolicited calls or messages were asked of phone owners, and the sending-money question follows reported exposure. We use published aggregates [4–6] as context.',
'The comparative design separates': 'We calculate weighted proportions as the sum of weights for positive responses divided by the sum of weights for eligible valid responses. Differences in percentages are descriptive. A weighted logistic regression of exposure includes digital payment use, gender, age, education, income quintile, workforce status and urbanicity. Income quintile is modeled as a linear ordinal term. Odds ratios describe conditional associations, not risk ratios or causation. Robust standard errors treat respondents as independent; because the released file lacks survey design identifiers, intervals and tests are exploratory and may understate or overstate uncertainty. No formal design-based significance claim is made.',
'The World Bank report documents device': 'Among phone owners, the survey records 219 reports of an unsolicited call or message asking for money, a weighted 9.4% of 2,145 phone owners. Of the 219, 45 reported sending money, a weighted 21.0% of those reporting such a request. These are self-reports, not verified losses, and the question does not establish that every request was fraudulent or connected to banking. The follow-up group is small and selected, so subgroup comparisons require caution.',
'The evidence suggests a testable opportunity': 'The observed adoption and phone-lock gaps suggest a testable opportunity for a bank or fintech partner to offer a multilingual digital payment safety companion. The proposed service would explain common scam patterns in short audio and visual lessons, use a simple checklist before a user approves a payment, and provide an easy route to the bank’s verified support and fraud-reporting channels. It should work on low-cost devices and be tested with first-time users, including women and small merchants. This is a concept based on survey patterns, not evidence of proven demand or effectiveness.',
'This paper relies on published aggregates': 'The survey is self-reported, cross-sectional and covers adults, not firms specifically. Digital payment use includes receiving payments and does not mean personal use of a mobile banking app. Phone owners are a selected subgroup; reported unsolicited requests may be legitimate, and a device lock is not a measure of resistance to social engineering. We cannot determine whether adoption or security behavior preceded an event. Recall bias, differences in phone activity and unmeasured factors may explain observed associations. The public file supplies weights but not the clustering or stratification identifiers needed for design-based uncertainty; model confidence intervals use an independent-observation approximation and should be treated cautiously.',
'Future work should acquire': 'Future work should obtain design information if available, reproduce design-based variance estimates, and study verified losses with consent and appropriate privacy safeguards. A randomized evaluation of safety interventions could help identify effects, provided the study protects users and measures unintended consequences. Entrepreneur-specific interviews would test whether the adult survey patterns translate into a business need.',
'India’s 2024 Findex figures show widespread': 'India’s 2024 Findex survey shows widespread account ownership alongside uneven digital payment use and device protection. Reported unsolicited requests were more common among phone-owning digital-payment users, even after adjustment in an exploratory model, but the association does not show that digital finance caused exposure. Low-friction safety education and support is a plausible entrepreneurial opportunity whose demand, effect and financial viability remain to be tested.'}
for para in d.paragraphs:
    for start,new in replacements.items():
        if para.text.startswith(start): para.text=new; break
    if para.text=='5 Proposed respondent level analysis': para.text='5 Respondent level analysis and results'

# Remove the old proposal body and its table between sections 5 and 6.
body=d._body._element
children=list(body); start=next(i for i,e in enumerate(children) if '5 Respondent level analysis and results' in ''.join(e.itertext())); end=next(i for i,e in enumerate(children) if '6 Entrepreneurial opportunity' in ''.join(e.itertext()))
for e in children[start+1:end]: body.remove(e)
anchor=next(p for p in d.paragraphs if p.text=='6 Entrepreneurial opportunity')
def before(text,style=None):
    elem=OxmlElement('w:p'); anchor._p.addprevious(elem); p=Paragraph(elem,anchor._parent)
    if style: p.style=style
    p.add_run(text)
    return p
before('5.1 Adoption and exposure', 'Heading 2')
before('Weighted estimates show that 89.0% of adults had an account and 48.5% made or received a digital payment (n = 3,000). Among 2,145 phone owners, 13.1% of digital-payment users (n = 1,283; 173 positive reports) reported receiving an unsolicited request for money, compared with 4.3% of non-users (n = 862; 46 positive reports), a difference of 8.8 percentage points. Digital payment use can include receiving money, while exposure is a call or text from an unknown person asking for money in the preceding 12 months; neither measure identifies a banking scam.')
before('5.2 Device security and demographic differences', 'Heading 2')
before('Among eligible phone owners, 57.7% had a device lock (n = 2,141; four uncertain responses excluded). The share was 67.4% for digital-payment users and 44.6% for non-users. A device lock protects access to a phone but may not prevent deceptive requests. Of the 219 people reporting an unsolicited request, 170 reported a lock and 49 did not. The weighted share reporting sending money was 25.0% and 8.9%, respectively (39 and 6 positive responses). The small, selected sample, potential confounding and unknown timing prevent any conclusion that locking increases or reduces the likelihood of sending money.')
before('Among women and men, weighted account ownership was 89.2% and 88.8%, while making or receiving a digital payment was 42.7% and 54.1%, respectively. Among phone owners, reported lock use was 50.3% for women (n = 919) and 62.7% for men (n = 1,222). Digital payment use was 34.8% in the lowest income quintile and 67.4% in the highest; corresponding lock use among phone owners was 40.6% and 75.2%. For those aged 60 or older, lock use among phone owners was 22.9% (n = 154), compared with 75.7% for ages 15–24 (n = 497). These measures use different denominators, and the results identify descriptive gaps rather than their causes.')
before('5.3 Adjusted association', 'Heading 2')
before('The weighted logistic model includes 2,145 phone owners and 219 exposure reports. Holding the included demographic variables constant, digital-payment users had 3.45 times the odds of reporting an unsolicited request compared with non-users (approximate 95% interval 2.24–5.30; independent-observation robust p < 0.001). The odds ratio is not a causal effect or a relative risk. Female respondents had lower adjusted odds than male respondents (odds ratio 0.47; approximate interval 0.32–0.69). These intervals do not account for clustering or stratification, so they are exploratory rather than design-based evidence. Other coefficients and complete subgroup counts are in the accompanying results files.')
before('The original age, income and educational categories, missingness rules, weighted denominators and model computation are documented in the accompanying analysis script. The data remain subject to the World Bank’s use and redistribution conditions [2].')
d.save('Group_31_Term_Paper_Completed.docx')
